.TH "DNSFMT" "1" "October 2026" "dnsfmt" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
dnsfmt \- format DNS zone files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]dnsfmt\f[R] [\f[B]\(enno\-inc\f[R]] [\f[B]\-o\f[R]
\f[I]ORIGIN\f[R]] [\f[B]\-r\f[R]] [\f[I]FILE\f[R]]\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]dnsfmt\f[R] formats zone files in the layout zoneomatic writes.
Without \f[I]FILE\f[R] (or with \f[CR]\-\f[R]) it reads standard input
and prints the result.
The ordering of the file is kept, as are all comments and
\f[CR]$\f[R]\-directives.
.PP
The layout rules:
.IP \(bu 2
owner names are written relative to the \f[CR]$ORIGIN\f[R] in effect and
aligned in a column (long names overflow it); a repeated owner is left
blank, but is written again after a comment, a blank line or a
directive;
.IP \(bu 2
a TTL column only appears for records whose TTL differs from the
\f[CR]$TTL\f[R] in effect; a TTL equal to it is redundant and dropped;
.IP \(bu 2
classes and types are upper\-cased and aligned; values are written as
they are, nothing is rewritten;
.IP \(bu 2
TXT strings are written quoted and escaped; long DNSKEY, DS and RRSIG
data is wrapped in parentheses;
.IP \(bu 2
the SOA record is written one field per line with its name as a comment,
the serial also with its date (Unix time or \f[CR]YYYYMMDDnn\f[R]);
.IP \(bu 2
comments stay next to the value they follow; blank lines are kept, runs
collapsed to one;
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]$ORIGIN\f[R] is always written fully qualified.
.PP
No semantic checks are done.
zoneomatic verifies its own writes with an independent parser;
\f[B]dnsfmt\f[R] does not.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[CR]\-o\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-origin\f[R] \f[I]ORIGIN\f[R]
the zone origin before the first \f[CR]$ORIGIN\f[R] directive; by
default taken from the file (\f[CR]$ORIGIN\f[R], or the SOA owner).
.TP
\f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-inc\f[R] / \f[CR]\-\-no\-inc\f[R]
bump the SOA serial (default).
A Unix\-time serial becomes the current time, always increasing; a
\f[CR]YYYYMMDDnn\f[R] serial is incremented by one.
.TP
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-replace\f[R]
rewrite the files in place (atomically) instead of printing.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
% cat <<\(aqEOF\(aq | dnsfmt \-\-no\-inc
$TTL 6H
$ORIGIN example.org.
\(at       IN      SOA     ns miek.miek.nl. 1282630067  4H 1H 7D 7200
                IN      NS  ns
example.org.            IN      NS  ns\-ext.nlnetlabs.nl.
www  21600 IN A 192.0.2.1
EOF
.EE
.PP
Returns:
.IP
.EX
$TTL 6H
$ORIGIN example.org.
\(at   IN SOA ns miek.miek.nl. (
    1282630067 ; serial  Tue, 24 Aug 2010 06:07:47 UTC
    4H         ; refresh
    1H         ; retry
    7D         ; expire
    7200       ; minimum
)
    IN NS  ns
    IN NS  ns\-ext.nlnetlabs.nl.
www IN A   192.0.2.1
.EE
.PP
The TTL of \f[CR]www\f[R] equals \f[CR]$TTL\f[R] and is dropped.
